=== FULL NODE-BY-NODE WALKTHROUGH (30 nodes, as read directly from live workflow JSON) ===

**Fetch pending RDA records and build client-sheet map (Steps 1-3c):**
1. Step 1 - Schedule Trigger: fires daily at hour 5.
2. Step 2 - Fetch Pending RDA Records1: uses n8n's NATIVE NocoDB node (unlike every other NocoDB call in this whole pipeline, which use raw HTTP requests) — `getAll`, sorted by RDA Timestamp, limit 100.
3. Step 2b - Filter Pending Records (Code): filters for `Approval Status !== 'Published'`, `Published Timestamp` null, `Client ID` present. **Contains explicit debug instrumentation left in production code** (`debug_count`, `debug_first`, a full `debug` object) — a maturity/hygiene signal distinguishing this workflow from the six per-record agents, none of which had leftover debug code.
   **Note: the `status !== 'Published'` check is likely vestigial.** Per RDA's own HOW document, `Approval Status` was simplified (Chat #97) to just 4 values (Pending/Approved/Modify/Not Accepted) — `'Published'` was removed as a possible value entirely (that concept now lives solely in the separate `Published Timestamp` field). This check can now never be false, so it's a harmless no-op — the real filtering work is done by the other two conditions.
4. Step 3 - Check Records Exist (IF): `pageInfo.totalRows > 0`. Only the TRUE branch is wired (to Step 3b) — sensible: if there's nothing pending, there's nothing further to do.
5. Step 3b - Fetch Client Config: GETs the Client Config table, limit 100, 30-second timeout.
6. STEP 3c — BUILD SHEET MAP: builds a `client_sheet_map` keyed by Client ID, containing sheet_id, tab_name, client_name, approval_email (sourced from each client's real "Approval Contact Email" field). Silently skips any client missing Sheet ID or Sheet Tab Name — reasonable defensive behavior.

**Fetch ALA records and build the per-client sheet-processing loop (Steps 4-6-loop):**
7. Step 4 - Fetch ALA Records: GETs ALA's table, limit 100, **no WHERE filter at all**. **Confirmed real scalability/correctness bug**: this fetches an arbitrary top-100 ALA rows (by whatever NocoDB's default ordering is) rather than the specific ALA records the actual pending RDA records reference. As ALA's table grows past a few hundred rows across multiple clients, this join will increasingly miss the records it actually needs, silently producing blank review text/platform/date fields for affected rows (confirmed later in the loop body — see Step 9). Note: this node's own upstream trigger was never shown connected from anything across any batch of the live audit — confirm directly how/when it actually fires relative to Step 1.
8. Step 5 - Build ALA Map (Code): builds `ala_map` keyed by ALA record Id, from whatever Step 4 returned.
9. STEP 6a — Explode Client Sheet Map (Code): converts the single client_sheet_map object into individual per-client items (fan-out), reading `STEP 3c`'s data by name reference.
10. Step 6-loop - Per Sheet Batch (SplitInBatches): loops through one client-sheet at a time. Output 0 (done) → empty in this specific batch (presumably a later convergence point not fully traced). Output 1 (item) → Step 6.
11. Step 6 - Fetch Existing Sheet Rows: n8n's NATIVE Google Sheets node, Service Account auth, retryOnFail true, alwaysOutputData true — reads the whole existing sheet for later dedup comparison, since Google Sheets has no server-side WHERE-style filtering the way NocoDB does.

**Dedup map and the main per-RDA-record loop (Steps 7-8a):**
12. Step 7 - Build Dedup Map (Code): reads existing sheet rows, extracts already-synced RDA Record IDs (column K / index 10 in a documented 12-column A:L layout) into a set for later dedup checks.
13. Step 7b - Merge All Data (Code): fans out one output item per pending RDA record, each carrying the raw rda_record, the full ala_map, the full existing_rda_ids set, client_id, sheet_id, tab_name.
14. Step 8a - Pre-Loop Platform Counter (Code): **confirmed broken.** Computes per-platform counts (`google_count`, `opentable_count`, etc.) and `t3_count` by reading `item.platform` and `item['Approval Status'] || item.status`, but neither field exists at that structural level on the items Step 7b actually produces (platform data lives nested inside `ala_map`'s values or inside `rda_record`, not at the top level). Result: every count is **always 0**, regardless of actual data. This has a directly visible downstream consequence — see Step 12d.
15. Step 8 - Loop RDA Records (SplitInBatches): the main per-record loop. Output 0 (done) → Step 11a. Output 1 (item) → Step 9. Confirmed by direct clarification: both the IF node's FALSE branch and the successful-append path (via Step 10b) loop back into this node to advance to the next item — this is deliberate, sensible loop closure, matching v2.0's documented "Step 9-check IF node... preventing silent execution halts" fix, not an accidental dead end.

**Per-record row-building and sheet append (Steps 9-10b, the loop body):**
16. Step 9 - Consolidated Logic (Code): the real per-record row-building logic.
    - Dedup check: returns `[]` immediately if this RDA record ID is already in `existing_rda_ids`.
    - ALA lookup: `ala_map[ala_id_raw] || ala_map[String(ala_id_raw)] || ala_map[parseInt(ala_id_raw)] || {}` — defensively handles type-mismatched keys, but falls back to `{}` silently if the ALA record simply isn't in the map at all (the real-world consequence of the Step 4 bug above).
    - Contains a documented fix: `.replace(/\\n/g, '\n')` on the proposed response — "fixes literal `\n` text not rendering in Spanish drafts," matching v2.0's changelog.
    - Builds the 12-column row: SCX Date, Review Date, Platform, Star Rating, Reviewer Handle, Review Text, Proposed Response, Status, Edited Response, ALA Record ID, RDA Record ID, Sync Status.
17. If (IF node, "Step 9-check" per v2.0's naming): checks `rda_record_id` notEmpty. TRUE → Step 9b. FALSE → loops back to Step 8 directly (skips the append). This is the documented v2.0 duplicate-handling fix.
18. Step 9b - Rate Limit Delay (Wait, 2 seconds): Google Sheets API rate-limit protection between appends.
19. Step 10a — Build Sheet Payload (Code): builds a manual Google Sheets API v4 `:append` REST payload (`valueInputOption=USER_ENTERED&insertDataOption=OVERWRITE` — matching v2.0's documented OVERWRITE-mode fix), constructing the 12-value row array. Has its own redundant empty-check (a third layer alongside Step 9's dedup return and the IF node — harmless, just more defensive layering than strictly needed).
20. Step 10b - Append Row to Sheet: raw HTTP POST directly to the Google Sheets API v4 endpoint (inconsistent with Step 6's use of the native node for reading, though not a functional bug). Has the multiple-stale-credentials issue noted above. Loops back to Step 8 after completing (confirmed by direct clarification).

**Live production data findings (from real sheet rows shared during the audit), confirmed via actual output, not just code inspection:**
21. **A star-symbol encoding/mojibake bug is leaking raw `"[5★]"`-style markup through the entire pipeline, uncaught.** Real sheet rows show Review Text values like `[5‚òÖ] Kenia was server...` — `‚òÖ` is the classic mojibake signature of a UTF-8 star emoji misread through a different encoding, likely originating in whatever CSV source feeds ALA. This matters specifically because ALA's own `Step 7 - Star Rating Extraction` has a regex (`/^\[(\d)[★*]?\]/`) meant to strip exactly this kind of prefix — but since the actual character is the garbled mojibake sequence, not a literal `★`, the regex doesn't match, and the bracketed rating notation survives all the way through ALA → EIP → ESS → HSI → BRA → RDA → this sheet, visible to the human approver.
22. **Real evidence that Claude isn't following RDA's exact required T1 opening pattern, and nothing downstream enforces it.** RDA's `Step 7a` requires the T1 opening to begin with exactly `"Thank you very much, [Name],"` or `"We appreciate you, [Name],"`. Both real example rows instead open with `"[Name], thank you so much..."` — a natural-sounding but non-compliant construction, consistently (2 for 2). RDA's own audit checklist (`Step 9b`) has no item checking for the literal required pattern, so this passes `audit_passed: true` regardless. Recommend either relaxing the prompt instruction to match observed natural output, or adding an audit-checklist item to actually enforce it — flag this for RDA's own HOW document too, not just this one.
23. **A small, directly-visible closing-signature punctuation bug.** One real row ends `Cheers!,` — comma immediately after an exclamation mark. Traced to RDA's `Step 9d`: `closing + ',\n' + brandSignature` unconditionally appends a comma regardless of whether the selected closing phrase already ends in punctuation (`"Cheers!"` in English, `"¡Saludos!"` in Spanish both have this issue). Simple fix: strip trailing punctuation before appending the comma, or don't add a comma at all and let each closing phrase carry its own correct punctuation. This is RDA's bug, not Sheet-Sync's, but it's only visible because of this sheet's real data — flag it in both docs.

**Reporting: daily digest email (Steps 11-12f):**
24. Step 11 - Build Summary (Code): simple summary object, dead-end connection — logging/debug purposes only.
25. Step 11a - Ensure Output (Code): recomputes a per-client `client_counts` breakdown (records_processed, plus the same broken platform/t3 fields as Step 8a — likely has the identical field-path bug, though the exact item shape reaching this node via the loop's "done" output wasn't fully confirmed). Fans out to both Step 11 and Step 12 (confirmed by direct clarification).
26. Step 12 - Log Completion (Set): adds completion_message/workflow_name/execution_timestamp.
27. Step 12a - Count Platform Records (Code): computes today's date and yesterday's date as ISO strings.
28. Step 12b1 - Fetch Previous Pending RDA: **same unfiltered `limit=100` pattern as Step 4** — a second instance of the same scalability risk, this time for the backlog/pending-count calculation.
29. Step 12b-filter - Filter Previous Pending (Code): computes `total_pending`, `pending_previous_days`, `new_today` per client. Contains `isPending = (approvalStatus === 'Pending' || approvalStatus === 'Pending-Elevated')` — this is the fourth confirmed sighting across RDA+Sheet-Sync of a reference to `'Pending-Elevated'`, a status value RDA's own schema no longer produces (removed in Chat #97's Approval Status simplification). **Important distinction: this specific instance is harmless**, since it's OR'd with the still-valid `'Pending'` value — unlike RDA's own `Step 18` and this workflow's `Step 8a`/`11a` `t3_count`, which check ONLY the now-impossible value with no fallback and are therefore genuinely non-functional. Don't conflate "stale reference" with "broken" across all four instances — only two of the four are actually broken.
30. Step 12c - Merge Counts Build Email Variables (Code): builds the final per-client email variable set.
    **Regarding the hardcoded email address (`approval_contact_email: 'miguel@solofella.com'`)**: initially flagged during the live-code audit as a critical bug (every client's notification going to one hardcoded personal address instead of each client's real configured contact). **This was substantially walked back after reading v2.0's explicit statement that this summary email is "internal operator-only" and that client-facing reporting is handled entirely by MRA** — if that's accurate, the hardcoded recipient may be correct, intentional behavior, not a defect. However, the variable is named identically to, and built from the same object as, each client's real `Approval Contact Email` field (`info.approval_email`, from `STEP 3c`) — a curious implementation choice if genuinely meant to always be internal-only rather than per-client. Recommend a direct, explicit confirmation of intent before either closing this out as "working as designed" or reopening it as a defect — don't guess either way.
31. Step 12d - Build Email Body (Code): builds a full HTML daily digest email. **Confirms the real-world consequence of the Step 8a/11a counting bug**: the platform-breakdown lines and the "⚠️ T3 elevated attention" warning block only render when their respective counts are `> 0` — since those counts are always 0, every daily email, for every client, permanently omits its most useful content, with no visible sign anything is wrong (the email still looks complete, just always empty in that section).
32. Step 12e - HTTP Send Email: POSTs to Brevo's transactional email API. **Resolves an open question from RDA's own audit**: RDA's `Step 18 - Approval Notification` builds email content but has no send mechanism — this node is where actual sending happens, as a daily digest rather than a per-record notification. Confirm explicitly whether RDA's `Step 18` output is used for anything at all, or is effectively superseded/vestigial given this daily-digest mechanism.
33. Step 12f - Log Email Delivery (Code): builds a delivery-status log object (checking for a `messageId` in Brevo's response) — not connected to any further node (chain-terminal), so this log is only visible in n8n's own execution history unless something else consumes it.

